@page "/guides/sqlite"

<DocPageMeta Title="SQLite for development and tests"
             Section="Guides"
             Order="50"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             Description="Run a Shift Framework app on SQLite, in memory or in a file, so development and tests need no SQL Server: register SQLite, create the schema, and know what works differently."
             Keywords="sqlite,in memory,database,development,tests,ensurecreated,shiftdbcontext,keep-alive,shared cache,sql server" />

<DocSection Title="When to use it">
    <p>
        A Shift Framework app normally runs on SQL Server. For development and automated tests you can run the same
        <code>DbContext</code> on SQLite instead, so nobody needs a SQL Server to start the app or run its tests.
        SQLite is a real relational database: unique indexes, transactions and raw SQL behave as they do on SQL Server.
    </p>
    <p>
        Which database runs in which environment is your choice, made in your own <code>AddDbContext</code> call. The
        framework does not check the environment.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning">
        SQLite allows one writer at a time and runs in one process. That is fine for development and tests. Do not
        use it for a production app that runs on several servers.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Register SQLite">
    <p>
        Add the <code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code> package to your API project and register your
        <code>DbContext</code> with <code>UseSqlite</code> instead of <code>UseSqlServer</code>. Pick one of two
        connection strings.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"// In memory: the data lives as long as the app and starts empty on every run.
builder.Services.AddDbContext<DB>(x => x.UseSqlite(""Data Source=MyApp;Mode=Memory;Cache=Shared""));

// In a file: the data is still there after a restart.
builder.Services.AddDbContext<DB>(x => x.UseSqlite(""Data Source=myapp.db""));")" />

    <p>
        A SQLite database in memory disappears as soon as its last connection closes, and every request closes its
        connection. So the first <code>ShiftDbContext</code> that opens a shared in-memory database keeps one extra
        connection open until the app stops. You do not write anything for this.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        Use the shared form above: a name, <code>Mode=Memory</code> and <code>Cache=Shared</code>. With
        <code>Data Source=:memory:</code>, or <code>Mode=Memory</code> without <code>Cache=Shared</code>, every
        connection gets a new, empty database. The framework then logs a warning once for that connection string,
        with the shared form to use.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Create the schema">
    <p>
        Your migrations are written for SQL Server, so do not apply them to SQLite. Create the SQLite schema from the
        model with <code>EnsureCreated</code>, after the app is built and before <code>app.Run()</code>. On a file it
        does nothing when the tables already exist.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"var app = builder.Build();

await using (var scope = app.Services.CreateAsyncScope())
    await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<DB>().Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();

app.Run();")" />

    <p>
        A schema made by <code>EnsureCreated</code> is not migrated later. When the model changes, delete the file (or
        restart, for a database in memory) and it is created again.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What works differently">
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>No history tables.</strong> SQLite has no temporal tables, so do not call <code>UseTemporal()</code>
            on SQLite. Features that read an entity's history (revisions) need SQL Server.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No schemas.</strong> A table in a schema, such as ShiftIdentity's <code>ShiftIdentity.Users</code>,
            is named <code>ShiftIdentity_Users</code> on SQLite, so it cannot collide with a table of yours with the
            same name.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Duplicate keys.</strong> When a save breaks a unique index, the error you get is SQLite's own
            message, not the friendlier message the framework writes for SQL Server.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Concurrent writes.</strong> Two requests that change the same row at the same moment are not
            queued: the second one fails. On SQL Server some framework writes wait for each other instead.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        ShiftIdentity adds a little to this: see <a href="/guides/identity-sqlite">ShiftIdentity on SQLite and the seed</a>.
    </p>
</DocSection>
